{
  "statement": "Copyright law must draw a bright line between functional indexing and expressive encoding because the failure to do so has created an accountability vacuum in which generative AI developers internalize the full expressive value of copyrighted works while bearing none of the liability for downstream harms. The New York Times v. OpenAI litigation, filed in December 2023, documented near-verbatim reproduction of copyrighted articles from GPT-4 — not paraphrases, not summaries, but passages retrievable almost word-for-word. That is not learning. That is storage with a generative interface. Section 230 intermediary immunity was designed for platforms that host and transmit user content, not for systems that ingest expressive works, encode them into model weights, and regenerate competing outputs. No existing liability regime assigns clear responsibility for this hybrid behavior. When legal structures allow commercial actors to externalize harm while internalizing value, the result is not innovation — it is the same structural failure pattern that left 346 people dead because Boeing and the FAA allowed certification gaps to persist until two planes fell out of the sky.\n\nThe doctrinal infrastructure for this distinction already exists. In Google v. Oracle, decided by the Supreme Court in 2021, the majority distinguished between copying functional API declarations — which served an interoperable, transformative purpose — and copying expressive implementing code. The Court's reasoning turned on whether the copying extracted expressive value for competitive deployment or served a different functional end. Search engine indexing received fair use protection in Kelly v. Arriba Soft (2003) and Perfect 10 v. Amazon (2007) precisely because it directed users back to original works rather than substituting for them. Generative AI training that encodes expressive content to produce competing outputs inverts that logic entirely. The doctrinal precedent is there. What is missing is statutory codification that prevents courts from reinventing this distinction one lawsuit at a time over the next two decades.\n\nUnrestricted expressive encoding degrades the creative commons that generative AI itself depends on. Shumailov et al. published in 2023 that models iteratively trained on synthetic data undergo progressive quality collapse — outputs degrade as AI-generated content replaces human-created content in training sets. Current estimates place AI-generated text at 10 to 15 percent of open web content, and that share is growing. If generative models freely encode expressive works, produce synthetic derivatives, and flood the ecosystem with those derivatives, the next generation of models trains on diluted inputs. This is a documented feedback loop, not a hypothetical. Copyright restrictions on expressive encoding function as a conservation mechanism — they preserve the human-created works that sustain both cultural production and AI capability. The strongest counterargument holds that this distinction is technically blurry because embedding models used for search also encode semantic features. That observation is accurate — and it strengthens the case for statutory clarity rather than undermining it. Precisely because the boundary is ambiguous in practice, the law must establish a default that protects expressive content and places the burden on developers to demonstrate functional purpose.\n\nThe burden of proof must fall on AI developers to demonstrate their copying is functional, not on creators to prove it is expressive. Every domain where scale creates asymmetric harm potential operates this way. Pharmaceutical companies bear the burden of demonstrating drug safety before market release. Aircraft manufacturers must certify airworthiness before a single passenger boards. The EU AI Act, which entered into force in August 2024, requires mandatory conformity assessments for high-risk AI systems before deployment. Mandatory training data disclosure, provenance logging, and independent audits are not technically infeasible — they are procedural requirements that AI developers resist because compliance costs money. My argument depends on two assumptions I want to name openly: first, that the functional-versus-expressive distinction can be operationalized through training pipeline transparency without requiring inspection of opaque model internals; second, that statutory codification will produce clearer compliance signals than case-by-case fair use adjudication. If either assumption fails, the enforcement architecture weakens. But the alternative — leaving the accountability vacuum unfilled while courts spend a generation sorting it out — is not caution. It is permission for unrestricted extraction from a shared resource without governance. That is the most expensive form of regulatory silence in democratic history.",
