    {"claim": "The New York Times v. OpenAI litigation documented near-verbatim reproduction of copyrighted articles from GPT-4 — not paraphrases, not summaries, but passages retrievable almost word-for-word.", "targets": [], "relationship": "supporting evidence for accountability vacuum"},
    {"claim": "In Google v. Oracle (2021), the Supreme Court distinguished between copying functional API declarations and copying expressive implementing code — the doctrinal infrastructure for this distinction already exists.", "targets": [], "relationship": "legal precedent establishing feasibility"},
    {"claim": "Shumailov et al. published in 2023 that models iteratively trained on synthetic data undergo progressive quality collapse — unrestricted expressive encoding degrades the creative commons that generative AI itself depends on.", "targets": [], "relationship": "supporting evidence for commons degradation"},
