  "statement": "The proposed distinction between functional indexing and expressive encoding doesn't protect creators — it hands the keys to the creative commons to whichever companies can afford licensing deals. That's the core problem with this framework, and everything else follows from it.\n\nThe Safetyist makes a genuinely compelling case that near-verbatim reproduction — the kind documented in NYT v. OpenAI — represents a real harm that copyright law should address. They're right that when a generative system regurgitates entire paragraphs of copyrighted text, calling that \"learning\" stretches the metaphor past breaking. The Skeptic sharpens this further by asking whether the functional/expressive line can actually be drawn in practice, given that the same embedding model can serve both retrieval and generation. These are serious arguments. But here's the tension: the Safetyist's own framework concedes the boundary is technically blurry, then proposes to codify it in statute anyway. For both positions to hold — that the line is ambiguous AND that we should write it into law — you'd need a legislative body capable of defining \"expressive encoding\" with enough precision to avoid catching semantic search, machine translation, and summarization in the same net. No legislature on Earth has demonstrated that capability.\n\nThe functional/expressive distinction will operate as a regressive compliance burden that entrenches incumbents. Google's search index already caches full pages, generates knowledge panels, and produces featured snippets that reproduce expressive content. Is that functional or expressive? Under this framework, every training pipeline requires a legal determination — and EU AI Act conformity assessments already run €200,000 to €400,000 per system. Microsoft, Google, and OpenAI have signed multi-billion dollar licensing deals with AP, Axel Springer, and News Corp. They can absorb this cost. Open-source labs like EleutherAI and academic researchers at public universities cannot. This distinction doesn't level the playing field — it tilts it permanently toward entities that already control both the models and the data. My argument depends on two assumptions: first, that output-level enforcement of verbatim reproduction is technically feasible at scale; second, that the compliance costs of input-level categorical restrictions fall disproportionately on smaller developers. If either is wrong, my framework weakens.\n\nThe better framework regulates outputs, not inputs. Verbatim reproduction is detectable at the output layer — automated plagiarism detection is mature technology, and C2PA provenance standards already exist for content authentication. Target the harm where it's measurable. This is the difference between regulating vehicle emissions and banning internal combustion. The Supreme Court in Google v. Oracle (2021) explicitly rejected bright-line rules in favor of context-sensitive fair use analysis. Kelly v. Arriba Soft (2003), Perfect 10 v. Amazon (2007), and Authors Guild v. Google (2015) all show courts drawing workable lines for technological copying without categorical prohibition. Statutory codification of an ambiguous distinction will lock in 2024's technical assumptions about how models work — and those assumptions have a shelf life measured in months, not decades.\n\nThe strongest version of the opposing case is that output-level enforcement can't catch non-verbatim market substitution — a model that paraphrases an entire novel or reproduces a photographer's style without copying any single image. That's a real gap. But the alternative — categorical input restrictions adjudicated pipeline-by-pipeline — is worse, because it's unenforceable for the entities causing harm and crushing for the entities that aren't. The music industry already ran this experiment: major label control over licensing created a system where three companies control access to the majority of recorded music. Shumailov et al.'s 2023 model collapse findings make this sharper — restricting training data access means only licensed incumbents avoid quality degradation, while everyone else trains on synthetic slop. You don't conserve a commons by privatizing it. Every month spent designing input-level categorical restrictions is a month where the data access moat hardens and the open development ecosystem shrinks. Who benefits from that delay? Not creators. Not the public. The companies that already signed the licensing deals.",
