rs at public universities cannot. This distinction doesn't level the playing field — it tilts it permanently toward entities that already control both the models and the data. My argument depends on two assumptions: first, that output-level enforcement of verbatim reproduction is technically feasible at scale; second, that the compliance costs of input-level categorical restrictions fall disproportionately on smaller developers. If either is wrong, my framework weakens.\n\nThe better framework regulates outputs, not inputs. Verbatim reproduction is detectable at the output layer — automated plagiarism detection is mature technology, and C2PA provenance standards already exist for content authentication. Target the harm where it's measurable. This is the difference between regulating vehicle emissions and banning internal combustion. The Supreme Court in Google v. Oracle (2021) explicitly rejected bright-line rules in favor of context-sensitive fair use analysis. Kelly v. Arriba Soft (2003), Perfect 10 v. Amazon (2007), and Authors Guild v. Google (2015) all show courts drawing workable lines for technological copying without categorical prohibition. Statutory codification of an ambiguous distinction will lock in 2024's technical assumptions about how models work — and those assumptions have a shelf life measured in months, not decades.\n\nThe strongest version of the opposing case is that output-level enforcement can't catch non-verbatim market substitution — a model that paraphrases an entire novel or reproduces a photographer's style without copying any single image. That's a real gap. But the alternative — categorical input restrictions adjudicated pipeline-by-pipeline — is worse, because it's unenforceable for the entities causing harm and crushing for the entities that aren't. The music industry already ran this experiment: major label control over licensing created a system where three companies control access to the majority of recorded music. Shumailov et al