r regime is a cautionary tale, not a model.\n\nThe tiered compliance proposal is smarter than flat regulation — the FAA Part 121/91 distinction is a real design pattern worth studying. But it breaks down at the classification step. In aviation, whether you're Part 121 or Part 91 is physically observable: scheduled airline versus private flight. In the proposed copyright framework, whether a technological copy is \"functional indexing\" or \"expressive encoding\" requires legal interpretation of computational processes that even machine learning researchers disagree about. Embedding extraction, attention-weight analysis, retrieval-augmented generation — these sit on a continuum, not in discrete buckets. A two-person startup doesn't need a cheaper compliance tier. They need to know which tier they're in without hiring a copyright attorney. That classification uncertainty is the moat. The EU DSM Directive's Article 3 research exemption illustrates this: researchers still report confusion about whether commercial-adjacent academic work qualifies, and the opt-out mechanism in Article 4 has generated litigation across member states rather than clarity.\n\nThe Safetyist claims non-verbatim market substitution is \"categorically undetectable\" at the output layer. That word — categorically — is doing enormous work. Stylometric analysis can already fingerprint authorial style with high accuracy. Embedding-space similarity measures detect semantic overlap below the verbatim threshold. Tools like Glaze and Nightshade demonstrate that output-layer detection is an active, rapidly improving frontier. Claiming categorical impossibility here is the same bet people made about spam detection in 2003 and deepfake detection in 2018. Both fields matured faster than the skeptics predicted. The 2025 Foundation Model Transparency Index, as documented in \"The 2025 Foundation Model Transparency Index,\" scored the industry at 40.69 out of 100 — which proves voluntary disclosure has failed. 