estically anchored. Domestic enforcement on these market-dominant actors sets the regulatory floor. Multilateral coordination raises it. Nuclear nonproliferation does not work perfectly across every jurisdiction, but no serious policymaker argues the United States should therefore abandon its own controls.\n\nWithout this layered architecture — copyright reform for individual works, discovery rights for verification, and a sectoral levy for aggregate displacement — we get one of two outcomes. Either copyright claims proliferate without enforcement capacity, burying courts in litigation that individual creators cannot afford. Or aggregate extraction proceeds uncompensated at profession-destroying scale, with the economic costs externalized onto the creators whose collective work made the models possible. The question is not whether the functional/expressive distinction is sufficient. No single legal instrument is. The question is whether we build the layered system that makes each instrument effective, or whether we let the perfect become the enemy of the enforceable.",
