ing frequency counts that are, by construction, less noisy than BLS aggregates?\n\nThe Safetyist's provenance logging proposal is the most operationally concrete mechanism anyone has put forward. That is worth saying plainly. But the verification gap is real and it is structural. The company controls the logging infrastructure. A firm that omits 10% of its training data from the provenance log cuts its levy obligation by 10%. Cryptographic hashing can prove a file was logged, but it cannot prove a file was not omitted. The \"International AI Safety Report 2026 Examines AI Capabilities, Risks, and Safeguards\" documents how automation bias leads people to trust system outputs even when those outputs are wrong — clinicians' tumor detection dropped 6% after months of AI-assisted procedures. The same dynamic applies to auditors trusting provenance logs. Completeness verification requires comparing the log against the actual training corpus, which means the auditor needs access to the raw data pipeline, not just the summary the company chooses to publish. Without that access — backed by independent subpoena power and funded from the AI service revenue stream rather than filing fees paid by the audited companies — provenance logging becomes another form of corporate self-reporting. Year one, the logs are thorough. Year three, they quietly degrade. That pattern repeats across every industry that has ever been asked to self-report compliance data.\n\nThe displacement fund without input-level accountability is a severance package, not a regulatory mechanism. It changes nothing about training practices. Compare it to pollution: an emissions tax that does not track who emits what is just a line item on a balance sheet. Companies budget for it and keep dumping. A provenance-logged levy creates a direct financial link between what you ingest and what you pay. That link changes behavior — it makes it cheaper to license and more expensive to scrape. The TAA analogy breaks down pre