onomically damaging harm vector in creative markets. When a generative model produces text that substitutes for a novelist's style without reproducing a single verbatim sentence, no plagiarism detector flags it. C2PA tracks content origin metadata but cannot detect whether a model's latent representations have internalized the expressive architecture of copyrighted works. Neural networks are epistemically opaque: no current interpretability method can map the causal pathway from a specific training work to a specific stylistic output. Output-only enforcement is an autopsy performed after the market damage is done. It is structurally analogous to regulating industrial pollution only by testing downstream water quality while permitting unlimited dumping upstream.\n\nThe harm is encoded at the input stage, not generated at the output stage. \"Alignment Whack-a-Mole: Finetuning Activates Verbatim Recall of Copyrighted Books in Large Language Models\" demonstrated that finetuning frontier LLMs on plot summaries enables verbatim reproduction of up to 85–90% of held-out copyrighted books. Safety alignment via RLHF did not prevent this extraction — finetuning bypassed it entirely. Machine unlearning methods, including gradient-based weight saliency and neural activation redirection, remain vulnerable to residual knowledge and relearning attacks, as documented in \"Machine Unlearning in Large Language Models: A Survey of Challenges and Methods.\" If the expressive content is encoded in model weights during training, and neither alignment nor unlearning can reliably remove it, then the only enforceable intervention point is before the encoding occurs. The Accelerationist's output-only framework asks courts and regulators to detect harms that current technology cannot reliably identify, while ignoring the one stage in the pipeline where the harm can be prevented.\n\nThe Skeptic is right that neither framework works without mandatory transparency and independent auditing. That critique strengthens the case for input-level regulation, not against it. The functional/expressive distinction creates a defined compliance checkpoint: what was copied, and for what declared purpose. Developers file a confidential training corpus disclosure with an independent auditor — analogous to SEC filings, not public release of proprietary data. The 2025 Foundation Model Transparency Index scored the industry at an average of 40.69 out of 100. That number is the baseline the enforcement regime must improve. Burden-shifting — where the developer must demonstrate functional purpose for copies that encode expressive content — plus mandatory disclosure plus independent auditing creates a three-layer enforcement architecture. Output-only enforcement, by contrast, requires continuous monitoring of all generated outputs across all deployment contexts, a task that is technically unbounded and practically unenforceable at scale.\n\nTwo assumptions anchor this argument. First, tiered compliance can be operationalized through training pipeline transparency without requiring inspection of opaque model internals — the audit targets the input manifest, not the weight matrices. Second, statutory codification produces clearer compliance signals than case-by-case fair use adjudication, which currently leaves creators and developers in legal uncertainty for years per case. The Accelerationist warns that licensing recreates the music industry's incumbency problem. The pre-licensing era tells the other half of that story. Napster and Limewire demonstrated that unrestricted copying destroyed creator compensation entirely. The Supreme Court in MGM v. Grokster held that distributing technology with the object of promoting infringement is actionable. The lesson is not that licensing is inherently regressive — it is that licensing must be designed with creator-protective structures and tiered access. Removing input-level protections recreates the conditions that collapsed the music industry's revenue by 50% between 1999 and 2010. The question is not whether to regulate at the input level, but whether to design that regulation so it scales fairly.",
