  "statement": "The functional/expressive distinction is necessary but insufficient — and the gap it leaves must be filled by a sectoral extraction levy, not by abandoning the distinction itself. The Accelerationist bundles two separate claims: that copyright cannot address aggregate profession-level displacement, and that this means the functional/expressive line is \"categorically insufficient\" and should be dropped. The first claim is right. The second does not follow. Copyright's inability to regulate macroeconomic displacement is not a defect of the functional/expressive distinction any more than building codes' inability to prevent earthquakes is a defect of structural engineering. The distinction does what copyright can do: it separates indexing from encoding, exempts the former, and restricts the latter. What copyright cannot do — compensate entire professions for the aggregate extraction of their collective capabilities — requires a second legal layer operating outside copyright's doctrinal scope.\n\nThat second layer is a statutory extraction levy indexed to training corpus volume and downstream revenue, administered by a collecting society with mandatory small-creator representation. The model already exists. ASCAP and BMI have administered compulsory music licensing in the United States since 1914, collecting over $1.5 billion annually from broadcasters who could never have negotiated individually with every songwriter. The UK's Public Lending Right compensates authors when libraries lend their books — a harm that copyright alone could not reach because lending is not copying. A training-data extraction levy operates on the same principle: AI companies that ingest the collective output of illustration, journalism, or translation pay into a fund proportional to the volume of works ingested and the revenue generated by models trained on them. The Copyright Labeling and Ethical AI Reporting Act (CLEAR Act) already establishes civil penalties of $5,000 per instance for failing to submit training-data notices, with an annual cap of $2,500,000 per person — proof that Congress can attach financial consequences to training-data transparency obligations. The levy extends this logic from penalty to compensation.\n\nThe Skeptic's point about the freelance illustrator who cannot afford a lawyer or determine whether her portfolio appeared in LAION-5B exposes a real enforcement failure that cryptographic manifests alone cannot fix. A manifest filed by the developer is vendor-authored documentation unless someone can verify it. The Skeptic's proposed mandatory discovery rights — allowing affected creators to audit manifests against actual training data — are the verification layer that prevents the safe harbor from collapsing into self-certification. Research published in \"Extracting Books from Production Language Models\" demonstrates that production LLMs memorize portions of their training data encoded in model weights, allowing extraction of in-copyright text. If the works are in the weights, affected parties need a legal mechanism to prove it. The full enforcement stack requires four layers: the functional/expressive distinction in copyright law, conditional safe harbors requiring pre-release manifests, mandatory discovery rights for independent verification, and the sectoral extraction levy for aggregate harms copyright cannot reach. Each layer addresses a different failure mode. Remove any one and the architecture has a gap that AI developers will route around.\n\nThe international enforcement gap does not justify abandoning domestic controls — it justifies building them first. OpenAI, Google, Anthropic, and Meta cannot credibly relocate their compute infrastructure, their capital markets access, or their talent pipelines. OpenAI and NVIDIA announced a partnership in September 2025 involving up to $100 billion in compute investment, as documented in \"2026 Antitrust Year in Preview\" by Wilson Sonsini. That capital is domestically anchored. Domestic enforcement on these market-dominant actors sets the regulatory floor. Multilateral coordination raises it. Nuclear nonproliferation does not work perfectly across every jurisdiction, but no serious policymaker argues the United States should therefore abandon its own controls.\n\nWithout this layered architecture — copyright reform for individual works, discovery rights for verification, and a sectoral levy for aggregate displacement — we get one of two outcomes. Either copyright claims proliferate without enforcement capacity, burying courts in litigation that individual creators cannot afford. Or aggregate extraction proceeds uncompensated at profession-destroying scale, with the economic costs externalized onto the creators whose collective work made the models possible. The question is not whether the functional/expressive distinction is sufficient. No single legal instrument is. The question is whether we build the layered system that makes each instrument effective, or whether we let the perfect become the enemy of the enforceable.",
