{
  "statement": "The safe harbor works when an independent auditor — not a plaintiff's lawyer — verifies the manifest. The Skeptic is right that a self-filed manifest without verification is vendor documentation. That's real. But the fix is not open-ended private litigation discovery, which would hand Getty Images and Universal Music a weapon to bleed every open-source developer dry with compliance costs. The fix is a standing audit body modeled on the Public Company Accounting Oversight Board: technically qualified auditors, rotating membership, publicly funded, with access to training data manifests and model internals under NDA. Creators file complaints that meet a threshold standard — substantial similarity between a registered work and model output. The auditor investigates, issues a public compliance report, and companies that refuse to cooperate lose their safe harbor automatically. No lawsuit needed to trigger verification. The lawsuit is the backstop for companies that stonewall the auditor, not the primary enforcement mechanism. This breaks the circularity the Skeptic identified: the auditor's report is what makes the system credible, not the threat of litigation.\n\nPrivate discovery rights sound democratic but function as a regressive tax. ASCAP and BMI — the existing collecting societies for music — distribute roughly 80% of royalties to the top 20% of earners. Hand discovery rights to private plaintiffs and the same pattern repeats: Sony Music can afford to depose Stability AI's engineers for six months, but the freelance illustrator in Tulsa cannot file a federal lawsuit. An independent auditor with anti-capture safeguards — rotating three-year terms, mandatory small-creator representation on the oversight board, five-year sunset review — democratizes verification. It works for the indie creator and the solo developer. The 2025 Foundation Model Transparency Index found the average transparency score across the AI ecosystem is 40.69 out of 100. That number proves we need structured, expert-driven disclosure — not a litigation free-for-all that only the well-capitalized can afford to play.\n\nThe Safetyist's extraction levy has a load-bearing assumption that collapses under inspection: that individual creator contributions to a training corpus can be measured and attributed. They cannot. Modern training pipelines deduplicate, tokenize, and augment data until the contribution of any single source is computationally intractable to isolate. The EU's text-and-data-mining directive under Articles 3 and 4 of the DSM Directive avoided this problem entirely by using opt-out rather than attribution-based compensation — because the EU's own technical assessments concluded attribution was not feasible at scale. If the levy distributes funds based on unmeasurable contributions, it becomes a slush fund that flows to whoever has the most registered works, which is incumbents. As \"2603.05175v1\" demonstrates, optimal regulatory mechanisms require that the set of non-compliant distributions form a credal set — a closed, convex set of probability measures — for the mechanism to achieve a perfect market outcome. The Safetyist's levy has no such formal structure; it substitutes administrative guesswork for measurable compliance triggers.\n\nThe convergence in this debate is wider than the disagreement. The Skeptic adopted the structural features of the hybrid framework: revenue-indexed compulsory licensing rates, small-creator representation on the rate-setting body, sunset clauses. The Safetyist endorsed the functional-expressive distinction and conditional safe harbors. What remains contested is narrow: auditor-based verification versus private discovery, and whether an extraction levy is administrable. That is a $244 billion industry — per Rep. Clarke and Sen. Markey's own figures — waiting on a verification mechanism that doesn't hand incumbents a litigation cudgel or create a new rent-extraction layer. The auditor model threads that needle. It gives creators a real enforcement path without requiring them to fund federal litigation, and it gives developers a clear compliance target without exposing proprietary architecture to competitors' lawyers.\n\nOne assumption I'm staking this on: that a PCAOB-style body can be structured to resist capture by the companies it audits. The PCAOB's own history is mixed — it took Sarbanes-Oxley's explicit statutory mandate and ongoing congressional oversight to keep it functional. A second assumption: that threshold filing standards can be calibrated to prevent both frivolous complaints and barriers to legitimate ones. Both are design problems, not conceptual objections. And here is the challenge for both opponents: DeepSeek shipped a competitive frontier model from China in January 2025 with no domestic compliance obligation whatsoever. Every month we spend building enforcement architecture that only binds domestically anchored companies is a month where creators' work is being trained on by actors we cannot reach. The auditor model is fast to stand up, cheap to operate, and binding on everyone who wants safe harbor in U.S. markets. What is the Safetyist's levy going to do about a training run in Hangzhou?",
