k. The Bureau of Labor Statistics already tracks occupational employment and wage data at this granularity. As \"The Regulatory Tide Goes Out\" documented, the first generation of AI frameworks was built ahead of the implementation infrastructure required to support them. A sector-level displacement fund avoids that sequencing trap because the measurement infrastructure already exists.\n\nOne last correction. The Skeptic cited GPT-4o's citation fabrication rate — nearly two-thirds — as evidence that auditor reliability is suspect. That statistic describes model output behavior: the model generating fake citations when asked to produce them. Training-data auditing does not ask the model to generate citations. It examines training logs, data pipelines, and applies membership inference externally. These are different technical operations with different reliability profiles. Conflating them is like questioning a building inspector's competence because the building's elevator sometimes goes to the wrong floor. If we cannot keep these distinctions clean, we will regulate the wrong thing. The question for this room is simple: if we can measure sector-level displacement today, why are we blocking progress on a per-work attribution problem nobody can solve?",
