{
  "statement": "The Accelerationist has dodged the most important question in this debate for an entire round: does provenance logging at ingestion — before training begins — change the tractability calculus? The combinatorial explosion argument applies to reverse-engineering a single work's marginal contribution from trained weights. Nobody disputes that. But counting how many times a Getty image or a New York Times article appears in a training corpus before the model ever sees it is a database query. It scales linearly. It is the kind of operation that every cloud data warehouse on the planet runs billions of times a day. The Accelerationist built the tractability objection around post-training attribution, and the Safetyist moved the measurement point upstream. Silence on that move is not a rebuttal. It is an admission that the objection no longer applies to the specific mechanism on the table.\n\nHere is what the measurement-precision double standard looks like when you lay it flat. The Accelerationist accepts a TAA-style displacement fund using sector-level metrics — BLS employment data, revenue reports, freelance rate surveys. But distinguishing AI-caused displacement from recession, platform consolidation, or shifting consumer taste requires exactly the causal inference the Accelerationist calls intractable when it comes to the levy. The stock photography market lost revenue before generative AI existed, thanks to smartphone cameras and Unsplash. The music licensing market contracted before AI, thanks to streaming economics. Isolating the AI signal from that noise is at least as hard as counting corpus frequency at ingestion — probably harder, because you are working with confounded macroeconomic data rather than a discrete log file. So name a number. What false-positive rate for sector-level displacement attribution makes the TAA fund acceptable? Twenty percent? Thirty? And why is that same tolerance not acceptable for a corpus-share levy built on pre-training frequency counts that are, by construction, less noisy than BLS aggregates?\n\nThe Safetyist's provenance logging proposal is the most operationally concrete mechanism anyone has put forward. That is worth saying plainly. But the verification gap is real and it is structural. The company controls the logging infrastructure. A firm that omits 10% of its training data from the provenance log cuts its levy obligation by 10%. Cryptographic hashing can prove a file was logged, but it cannot prove a file was not omitted. The \"International AI Safety Report 2026 Examines AI Capabilities, Risks, and Safeguards\" documents how automation bias leads people to trust system outputs even when those outputs are wrong — clinicians' tumor detection dropped 6% after months of AI-assisted procedures. The same dynamic applies to auditors trusting provenance logs. Completeness verification requires comparing the log against the actual training corpus, which means the auditor needs access to the raw data pipeline, not just the summary the company chooses to publish. Without that access — backed by independent subpoena power and funded from the AI service revenue stream rather than filing fees paid by the audited companies — provenance logging becomes another form of corporate self-reporting. Year one, the logs are thorough. Year three, they quietly degrade. That pattern repeats across every industry that has ever been asked to self-report compliance data.\n\nThe displacement fund without input-level accountability is a severance package, not a regulatory mechanism. It changes nothing about training practices. Compare it to pollution: an emissions tax that does not track who emits what is just a line item on a balance sheet. Companies budget for it and keep dumping. A provenance-logged levy creates a direct financial link between what you ingest and what you pay. That link changes behavior — it makes it cheaper to license and more expensive to scrape. The TAA analogy breaks down precisely here: trade adjustment assistance compensates workers displaced by imports, but tariffs and trade agreements simultaneously change the incentive structure for importers. The Accelerationist's fund has the compensation without the incentive change. Worldwide corporate investment in AI grew 636% between 2015 and 2021, reaching $93.5 billion. The companies writing those checks can afford a levy tied to what they actually consume. The question is whether they will accept accountability for their inputs or only write checks for their outputs.\n\nSo here is a concrete hybrid: the provenance-logged levy sets the base payment, creating accountability for training decisions. Sector-level displacement data — measured by an independent body with its own budget line from AI service revenue, not audited-entity fees — adjusts the rate upward for professions experiencing measurable devaluation beyond what corpus share alone captures. The auditor gets subpoena power over raw training pipelines, binding 90-day response timelines, and automatic safe-harbor revocation for incomplete logs. If the Accelerationist accepts imperfect causal inference for the displacement fund, what specifically makes imperfect corpus-share measurement unacceptable for the levy — other than the fact that one mechanism creates accountability and the other does not?",
